# F5-style single source: the verification-command vocabulary shared by the
# bash engine (phase-graph.sh sources this file) and the console's runner
# (viewer/server/runner/verify-env.ts parses the same file). Change a list
# only here — a drift test on the viewer side pins the two readers together.
#
# NOT here on purpose: the runner's read-only command allowlist (VERBS /
# REACHES_OUT in viewer/server/runner/verify.ts). Those carry per-verb
# judgement — closures over subcommands and flags — and flattening them into
# an env line would fake a single source while hiding the half that matters.

# Leads whose meaning depends on the working directory. A §Verification
# command that starts with one of these and declares no **Verify in:** runs
# at the repository root, where it may test the wrong tree (lint F18 warns at
# plan time; the runner's boarding preflight warns again at run time).
CWD_SENSITIVE="docker docker-compose pnpm npm yarn task make just pytest go cargo alembic vitest jest tsc node"

# Names never worth a resolution warning: shell builtins, keywords and
# always-present tools. Lint F17 and the runner's boarding preflight both
# skip them. The keywords matter for the runner's file probe — `if` and `do`
# are not files on any PATH and must not read as "missing binaries".
PREFLIGHT_SKIP="cd true false echo printf test pwd env which bash sh command export set time if then fi elif else for while until do done case esac"

# Commands that by construction wait on a clock OUTSIDE the session. One
# alternation, two readers, two moments:
#
#   - plan time — lint F16 (phase-graph.sh) warns that a §Verification built
#     from these will hold a session for the full external duration;
#   - run time  — the console's stall detector (viewer/server/runner/liveness.ts,
#     fed by verify-env.ts) sees the same shapes in an OPEN Bash call and turns
#     a session squatting an exclusive lock into an honest `waiting-external`
#     park.
#
# This was one hardcoded list until the second reader existed, which is the
# drift this file's rule is for: a vocabulary the lint knows and the detector
# does not is a plan-time warning about a runtime nobody watches. The drift
# test (viewer/test/verify-env.test.ts) pins both readers and the TS fallback.
#
# SINGLE-quoted, unlike the two lists above, because the value carries
# backticks — the negated spans that stop a match crossing a markdown
# code-span boundary — and inside double quotes bash would run them as command
# substitution.
#
# Dialect-neutral on purpose: every construct here is valid in BOTH POSIX ERE
# (`grep -oE`) and JavaScript `RegExp`, which is what lets one string drive two
# readers in two languages. That is why ` --watch` carries a literal space
# rather than `[[:space:]]`, which JS has no equivalent for — both readers
# collapse whitespace runs to single spaces before matching (bash `tr`, and the
# runner's own `summarise`), so the space is exactly equivalent and portable.
#
# A LOOP is a wait only when it holds a clock (control-tower phase 47, #52):
# a `sleep`, a `wait`, a timed `read`, or a network verb — in its condition or
# its body. The two loop arms below are one rule spelled twice, clock before
# the `do` and clock after it. A counter that waits on nothing (twenty
# `printf`s into a throwaway container) used to match by shape alone, was
# refused, and the refusal outlived itself long enough to kill the session. A
# loop cannot borrow a clock from a statement after its own `done`: both
# readers split the text there before they match (`external_wait_hit` below,
# `externalWaitHit` in verify-env.ts).
EXTERNAL_WAIT='gh run watch|gh pr checks[^`]*--watch| --watch([^A-Za-z]|$)|task deploy|sleep [0-9]{3,}|sleep [6-9][0-9]([^0-9]|$)|sleep [0-9]+[mh]|(until|while (\[\[? |test |\(\( |! |(true|:) *;))[^`]*([ ;&|(!]sleep [0-9"$]|[ ;&|(]wait( |;|$)|[ ;&|(]read -t|[ ;&|(!](gh|aws|kubectl|ssh|scp|rsync|vercel|terraform|flyctl|fly|gcloud|az|doctl|heroku|curl|wget|nc) |[ ;&|(!]git (fetch|pull|push|ls-remote|clone)|https?://)[^`]*(; *| )do( |;|$)|(until|while (\[\[? |test |\(\( |! |(true|:) *;))[^`]*(; *| )do[^`]*([ ;&|(!]sleep [0-9"$]|[ ;&|(]wait( |;|$)|[ ;&|(]read -t|[ ;&|(!](gh|aws|kubectl|ssh|scp|rsync|vercel|terraform|flyctl|fly|gcloud|az|doctl|heroku|curl|wget|nc) |[ ;&|(!]git (fetch|pull|push|ls-remote|clone)|https?://)|while sleep [^`]+; *do|watch -n|aws [a-z0-9-]+ wait |kubectl rollout status|docker[ -]compose logs -f|docker[ -]compose up( |$)|tail -f'
# The ONE carve-out from the list above, applied by REMOVAL: every match of this
# is deleted from the text before EXTERNAL_WAIT is asked, so a shape that would
# otherwise match cannot. It exists for exactly one pair — `docker compose up`
# blocks in the foreground for ever and `docker compose up -d` returns at once —
# and a carve-out is the honest way to say that, because the two commands share
# a prefix and no single alternation can distinguish them.
#
# Removal rather than a second `grep -v`: the question is asked of a LINE that
# may hold several commands, so "does this line also contain an allowed shape"
# is the wrong question. Deleting the allowed span and re-asking is the right
# one, and it is the same operation in `sed -E` and JS `String.replace`.
#
# The session's own `phase-outcome.sh` line is the other span removed: its
# arguments are words ABOUT a wait, never a wait. A double-quoted argument is
# taken whole, so a `;` inside a `--what` no longer ends the span and leaves
# the rest of the sentence to be judged as code (AUD-34).
EXTERNAL_WAIT_ALLOW='docker[ -]compose up (-d|--detach)|phase-outcome\.sh([^;&|"]|"[^"]*")*'

# The ONE bash reader of the two values above: lint F16 calls it, and the drift
# test asks it about the same inputs it asks `externalWaitHit` (verify-env.ts).
# Reads the text on stdin; prints the first fragment that matches, or nothing.
# Four steps, in the JS twin's order: fold (a backslash-newline joins, any other
# newline is a `; `, tabs and space runs are one space); carve the allowed
# spans out; split after every `done` KEYWORD (the first word of a command, so
# `build/done.flag` stays whole), so a loop is judged inside its own end;
# match. bash 3.2 and BSD tools: no `mapfile`, and the newline in the second
# `sed` is a literal one, which both seds accept.
external_wait_hit() {
  local carve q
  carve=$(printf '\001')
  q="'"
  awk '{ if (sub(/\\$/, "")) printf "%s ", $0; else printf "%s; ", $0 }' \
    | sed 's/; $//' | tr '\t' ' ' | tr -s ' ' \
    | sed -E "s${carve}${EXTERNAL_WAIT_ALLOW}${carve}${carve}g" \
    | sed -E "s/(^|[;&] *)done([ ;&|)${q}\"]|\$)/\1done\\
\2/g" \
    | grep -oE "$EXTERNAL_WAIT" | head -1 | sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//; s/[[:space:]]*$//'
}

# Bring-up, not proof. A §Verification line matching one of these installs or
# starts something the phase needs BEFORE it can be proved — it is a preamble,
# and the format now has a home for it (`- **Setup:**`, references/plan-format.md
# §6). Lint F22 says so at plan time; the runner runs Setup commands with
# `check:false` so a preamble can never colour a phase red.
#
# `docker run ` is deliberately absent, and the reason is the one rule this list
# has to obey: F22 tells an author to MOVE a command into `- **Setup:**`, so
# every shape on this list must be a shape that bullet will actually run.
# `docker run -d --name pg postgres:17` is not — the runner judges `docker run`
# by the command it carries, and an image name is not a command, so it is
# refused in BOTH lanes. Advising a move that silently drops the command is
# worse than not advising it. `docker compose up -d` is the shape to reach for.
#
# Deliberately NOT a synonym for EXTERNAL_WAIT: a shape can be both (a
# foreground `docker compose up` waits AND is bring-up) and the two advisories
# say different things about it, so both firing on one line is correct rather
# than duplicated.
SETUP_LEADS='docker[ -]compose up|docker start |npm ci|npm install|pnpm install|yarn install|bundle install|pip install|uv sync|poetry install|terraform init|alembic upgrade|minikube start|kind create cluster|vagrant up|sleep [0-9]'

# A §Verification line whose exit code reads FLEET-WIDE state — every plan,
# branch and working tree in the checkout, or every process on the machine —
# rather than what this plan did (control-tower phase 62, #47). Lint F32 warns
# on one at plan time; nothing refuses it, because the command is safe and
# sometimes it is exactly what an author means. The measured cases: a ship
# phase verified with `task hygiene -- --offline`, which in a shared checkout
# can never exit 0 while the run is live — the console's own minted
# `pe/<slug>` branches (one pinned by the run's worktree) and three other live
# plans' trees made 31 of its 33 findings — so the phase shipped, declared
# `blocked`, and waited for a person; and a heap check written
# `ps -axo pid,command | grep -E 'phase-console|viewer/server' | …`, which
# matched EVERY console on the machine and reported another one's
# `--max-old-space-size` as this one's (#71's PEH-3, phase 89).
#
# One reader, bash only (the runner does not refuse these, so it has no twin):
# `fleet_wide_hit` below splits the text into commands, keeps those matching
# FLEET_WIDE_READS and drops those FLEET_WIDE_SCOPED shows read ONE thing.
#
# A process read is `ps` as a command word whose output reaches a grep, through
# any stages between (`docker compose ps` lists one project's services and is
# not one; `pgrep` is not judged, because its pattern IS its scope and no lint
# can tell a path unique to one instance from a word every instance shares).
#
# SCOPED holds only what the tasks themselves accept (read from the fleet's
# Taskfile.yml and scripts/fleet-hygiene.sh, phase 89 — phase 62's `--plan`,
# `--repos`, `--root` and `ROOT=` were forms nothing accepts):
#   - `task hygiene -- <args>` hands its args to fleet-hygiene.sh, which takes
#     `--offline`/`--no-prs`, `--remote-only`, `--json` and `--stale-days N` —
#     every one still the whole fleet — and exits 2 on anything else, `--plan`
#     and `--repos` included. Only `--check-tag <repo> <tag>` and
#     `--list-tag-allowlist` answer from a constant and read no repository.
#   - `task drift` reads no arguments at all (its command never names
#     CLI_ARGS, so `-- --root …` is dropped), and a `ROOT=` prefix is
#     overwritten by the scripts' own `HUB_ROOT=$(cd …)`: nothing scopes it.
#     One gate (`task drift:pins`) is not a read here — the `:` keeps it out —
#     and neither is task's own `task -d <dir> drift`, which runs another
#     checkout's gates against that checkout.
#   - a process read is scoped when it names one process by pid: `ps -p <pid>`
#     (`-fp <pid>`, `--pid`), the pid from the instance's own port
#     (`lsof -tiTCP:<port> -sTCP:LISTEN`) or pid file.
FLEET_WIDE_READS='(^|[ ])task (hygiene|drift)([^A-Za-z0-9:_-]|$)|(^|[({!"]|(^|[ ])(if|then|else|do|time|sudo|exec)) ?ps( [^|]*)?(\|[^|]*)*\| ?(e|f)?grep( |$)'
FLEET_WIDE_SCOPED='(^|[ ])task hygiene -- ([^|]* )?--(check-tag|list-tag-allowlist)( |$)|(^|[({!" ])ps [^|]*(-[A-Za-z]*p ?[0-9$"(]|--pid[ =])'
# Reads §Verification text on stdin; prints the first fleet-wide command, whole,
# or nothing. A command is a backticked span, a fenced line, or one part of a
# `&&` / `||` / `;` chain — so a scoping flag on one command never excuses
# another. A single `|` does not split: a pipeline is one command, and the
# `grep` a process read ends in is part of it. bash 3.2 and BSD tools; the
# newline in the `sed` is a literal one.
fleet_wide_hit() {
  tr '`\t' '\n ' \
    | sed -E 's/(&&|\|\||;)/\
/g' \
    | sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//; s/[[:space:]]*$//' | tr -s ' ' \
    | grep -E "$FLEET_WIDE_READS" | grep -vE "$FLEET_WIDE_SCOPED" | head -1
}
